# A container method called on something that is not a container has to fail
# loudly: xs[0] is an int, so `.append` is a TypeError the runtime raises.
#
# It used to be a segfault. The list and set appends write into a union that an
# int's bits also fill, so appending to one read the number as a length and a
# capacity and wrote through the pointer found there; append_type_error.exit
# records the status a raised error gives (1), not a crash's 139.
#
# Nothing is printed before the failing statement: the oracle drops buffered
# output when a program dies (see plan.md), so a case that printed first could
# not compare equal on stdout across the engines.

xs = [1, 2, 3]
xs[0].append(4)
print("not reached")
